{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792370886897,"sessionID":"ses_eae696937ffe0wN6WZCrNY9lxd","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b870c3930f1368d1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\93rob\\Documents\\GitHub\\SpaceFace\\src\\core\\perfCounters.js","limit":80},"output":"<path>C:\\Users\\93rob\\Documents\\GitHub\\SpaceFace\\src\\core\\perfCounters.js</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: // Tier-1 deterministic performance counters.\n2: //\n3: // WHY THIS IS SEPARATE FROM perfRuntime.js\n4: // ----------------------------------------\n5: // `perfRuntime.js` measures DURATIONS. Every number it produces is a wall-clock span, so every\n6: // number it produces is only as trustworthy as the machine it was captured on. This workstation\n7: // routinely runs many concurrent agents; a millisecond captured here is informational, never\n8: // evidence.\n9: //\n10: // This module measures COUNTS, SIZES and IDENTITIES — never durations. \"47 shader programs were\n11: // linked after the first playable frame\" means exactly the same thing on an idle machine and on a\n12: // machine running twelve agents, because CPU contention cannot change an integer. That property is\n13: // the entire point: it is what makes a counter delta between two commits a real regression rather\n14: // than noise, and therefore bisectable.\n15: //\n16: // Do not add a timing field to this module. If you need timings, they belong in perfRuntime (Tier 2)\n17: // and must be captured in a separate run — the GL wrappers that feed these counters deoptimise the\n18: // hottest calls in the frame, so counts and timings taken together would be instrument-distorted.\n19: //\n20: // ZERO COST WHEN OFF\n21: // ------------------\n22: // This is a performance tool; it must not be a performance problem. Every entry point begins with a\n23: // single boolean read and returns. There is no allocation, no closure creation, no string building\n24: // and no array push on the disabled path — `test/perf-counters.test.mjs` proves it by driving 5,000\n25: // disabled frames and asserting the event list, the histogram and every total are still untouched.\n26: // The heavier observers (GL context wrappers, MutationObserver) are not merely branched over but\n27: // INSTALLED ON ENABLE, so when disabled they do not exist as call sites at all.\n28: //\n29: // DETERMINISM\n30: // -----------\n31: // Not every counter here is reproducible run to run, and pretending otherwise would make the\n32: // equivalence gate either vacuous or permanently red. `DETERMINISTIC_FIELDS` is the explicit\n33: // allowlist the gate compares; everything else is reported but excluded. See\n34: // design/program/roadmap/PERF_INSTRUMENTATION_INVENTORY.md §4 for why each exclusion is there.\n35: \n36: export const PERF_COUNTERS_SCHEMA = 'spaceface.perfCounters.v1';\n37: \n38: // Counter fields, in report order. Kept as one frozen list so the per-frame reset is a fixed loop\n39: // over known keys rather than an Object.keys() allocation on every frame.\n40: export const COUNTER_FIELDS = Object.freeze([\n41:   // A — shader programs. The highest-value family: a link is a 50-300 ms main-thread stall.\n42:   'shaderLinks',\n43:   'shaderCompiles',\n44:   // B — render targets.\n45:   'renderTargetAllocations',\n46:   'renderTargetResizes',\n47:   // C — textures.\n48:   'textureUploads',\n49:   'textureSubUploads',\n50:   'mipmapGenerations',\n51:   // D — buffers.\n52:   'bufferFullUploads',\n53:   'bufferPartialUploads',\n54:   'bufferUploadBytes',\n55:   // E — draw and state.\n56:   'drawCalls',\n57:   'drawInstancedCalls',\n58:   'programSwitches',\n59:   'textureBinds',\n60:   // H — DOM.\n61:   'domMutations',\n62:   'domChildListMutations',\n63:   'domAttributeMutations',\n64:   'domCharacterDataMutations',\n65:   'layoutReads',\n66:   'longTasks',\n67:   // J — sim-core causal work. Per-tick loop costs, counted where the loop runs.\n68:   'systemInvocations',\n69:   'entityVisits',\n70:   'queryCandidates',\n71:   'collisionPairs',\n72:   // K — package decode and scene-graph work. The render-package route's stall class.\n73:   'packageDecodes',\n74:   'runtimeSemanticCompiles',\n75:   'graphCloneOperations',\n76:   'graphNodesCloned',\n77:   'graphTraversals',\n78:   'graphNodesVisited',\n79:   // K2 — the flat instance plan that REPLACES clone+traverse. Counted as its own family so\n80:   // a report shows elimination rather than a silent relabelling of the same work.\n\n(Showing lines 1-80 of 770. Use offset=81 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"// Tier-1 deterministic performance counters.\n//\n// WHY THIS IS SEPARATE FROM perfRuntime.js\n// ----------------------------------------\n// `perfRuntime.js` measures DURATIONS. Every number it produces is a wall-clock span, so every\n// number it produces is only as trustworthy as the machine it was captured on. This workstation\n// routinely runs many concurrent agents; a millisecond captured here is informational, never\n// evidence.\n//\n// This module measures COUNTS, SIZES and IDENTITIES — never durations. \"47 shader programs were\n// linked after the first playable frame\" means exactly the same thing on an idle machine and on a\n// machine running twelve agents, because CPU contention cannot change an integer. That property is\n// the entire point: it is what makes a counter delta between two commits a real regression rather\n// than noise, and therefore bisectable.\n//\n// Do not add a timing field to this module. If you need timings, they belong in perfRuntime (Tier 2)\n// and must be captured in a separate run — the GL wrappers that feed these counters deoptimise the\n// hottest calls in the frame, so counts and timings taken together would be instrument-distorted.\n//\n// ZERO COST WHEN OFF","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\Users\\93rob\\Documents\\GitHub\\SpaceFace\\src\\core\\perfCounters.js","text":"// Tier-1 deterministic performance counters.\n//\n// WHY THIS IS SEPARATE FROM perfRuntime.js\n// ----------------------------------------\n// `perfRuntime.js` measures DURATIONS. Every number it produces is a wall-clock span, so every\n// number it produces is only as trustworthy as the machine it was captured on. This workstation\n// routinely runs many concurrent agents; a millisecond captured here is informational, never\n// evidence.\n//\n// This module measures COUNTS, SIZES and IDENTITIES — never durations. \"47 shader programs were\n// linked after the first playable frame\" means exactly the same thing on an idle machine and on a\n// machine running twelve agents, because CPU contention cannot change an integer. That property is\n// the entire point: it is what makes a counter delta between two commits a real regression rather\n// than noise, and therefore bisectable.\n//\n// Do not add a timing field to this module. If you need timings, they belong in perfRuntime (Tier 2)\n// and must be captured in a separate run — the GL wrappers that feed these counters deoptimise the\n// hottest calls in the frame, so counts and timings taken together would be instrument-distorted.\n//\n// ZERO COST WHEN OFF\n// ------------------\n// This is a performance tool; it must not be a performance problem. Every entry point begins with a\n// single boolean read and returns. There is no allocation, no closure creation, no string building\n// and no array push on the disabled path — `test/perf-counters.test.mjs` proves it by driving 5,000\n// disabled frames and asserting the event list, the histogram and every total are still untouched.\n// The heavier observers (GL context wrappers, MutationObserver) are not merely branched over but\n// INSTALLED ON ENABLE, so when disabled they do not exist as call sites at all.\n//\n// DETERMINISM\n// -----------\n// Not every counter here is reproducible run to run, and pretending otherwise would make the\n// equivalence gate either vacuous or permanently red. `DETERMINISTIC_FIELDS` is the explicit\n// allowlist the gate compares; everything else is reported but excluded. See\n// design/program/roadmap/PERF_INSTRUMENTATION_INVENTORY.md §4 for why each exclusion is there.\n\nexport const PERF_COUNTERS_SCHEMA = 'spaceface.perfCounters.v1';\n\n// Counter fields, in report order. Kept as one frozen list so the per-frame reset is a fixed loop\n// over known keys rather than an Object.keys() allocation on every frame.\nexport const COUNTER_FIELDS = Object.freeze([\n  // A — shader programs. The highest-value family: a link is a 50-300 ms main-thread stall.\n  'shaderLinks',\n  'shaderCompiles',\n  // B — render targets.\n  'renderTargetAllocations',\n  'renderTargetResizes',\n  // C — textures.\n  'textureUploads',\n  'textureSubUploads',\n  'mipmapGenerations',\n  // D — buffers.\n  'bufferFullUploads',\n  'bufferPartialUploads',\n  'bufferUploadBytes',\n  // E — draw and state.\n  'drawCalls',\n  'drawInstancedCalls',\n  'programSwitches',\n  'textureBinds',\n  // H — DOM.\n  'domMutations',\n  'domChildListMutations',\n  'domAttributeMutations',\n  'domCharacterDataMutations',\n  'layoutReads',\n  'longTasks',\n  // J — sim-core causal work. Per-tick loop costs, counted where the loop runs.\n  'systemInvocations',\n  'entityVisits',\n  'queryCandidates',\n  'collisionPairs',\n  // K — package decode and scene-graph work. The render-package route's stall class.\n  'packageDecodes',\n  'runtimeSemanticCompiles',\n  'graphCloneOperations',\n  'graphNodesCloned',\n  'graphTraversals',\n  'graphNodesVisited',\n  // K2 — the flat instance plan that REPLACES clone+traverse. Counted as its own family so\n  // a report shows elimination rather than a silent relabelling of the same work.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":80,"totalLines":770,"truncated":true}},"title":"src\\core\\perfCounters.js","time":{"start":1792370886822,"end":1792370886887}},"id":"prt_151a146fc001Dm11lteYySqTtx","sessionID":"ses_eae696937ffe0wN6WZCrNY9lxd","mess